**Case and dockets:** Williams v. Taylor (Michael Wayne Williams); No. 99-6615.
**Event and date:** Merits decision; 2000-04-18; October Term 1999.
**Result:** Affirmed in part, reversed in part, and remanded; psychiatric-report hearing denial affirmed 8–1; two relationship-based hearing barriers set aside by all nine, with eight fixing diligence and cause and Stone-Zsela seeking further diligence inquiry.
**Version / lineage:** Original decision; no prior adjudication replaced. Repository verification and commitment reserved to the operator.

**Source chunk:** OT_1999CHUNK5.

## Event, participation and entering law

Argued February 28, 2000; decided April 18, 2000; certiorari review of the Fourth Circuit's August 2, 1999 judgment, 189 F.3d 421. The granted hearing question concerns section 2254(e)(2)'s opening condition and diligent state-court efforts. All nine participated at argument and decision; no disqualification is supplied. Nine meet the quorum; five votes suffice. Research cutoff: April 18, 2000. The federal application postdates AEDPA's April 24, 1996 enactment. Terry Williams, an independent same-day case, supplies no entering law.

The frozen neutral distinguishes three appellate hearing claims: Cruse's psychiatric report; juror Stinnett's undisclosed former marriage to witness Meinhard; and prosecutor Woodson's related prior representation and alleged nondisclosure. The known transcript references and accessible court-file report differ from the concealed relationships and denied investigative assistance. The affidavits are not resolved credibility findings. Williams concedes inability to satisfy section 2254(e)(2)(B). The separate plea-agreement claim was decided on its merits under section 2254(d), not as another appellate hearing-bar application. Presence-comment, assistance/confidentiality and filing-period rulings remain unreviewed.

The entering-law slice and public current-term supplement preserve Keeney's actual deliberate-bypass former-law rule, Lindh's provision-specific timing, and Weeks's independent state-merits predicate. Coleman claim default, factual development and the statutory merits gate are different inquiries. The lower court's alternative cause reasoning cannot be sustained by importing a historical Keeney holding that is not law here. The reconstructed-source procedure supplies no missing adverse fact or litigant default; supported published recitations suffice for the bounded disposition.

## Stone

The approved October 31 position calls for vacatur and remand for a hearing if the district court finds the required diligent state effort, while preserving remaining hearing and merits conditions. Stone joins the legal rule attributing failure to the applicant, measured by reasonable timely efforts in light of available information; strategic inaction and unexplained neglect trigger the statute. He accepts its enacted exceptions and does not confine the new text to Keeney's former deliberate-bypass rule.

His express remand of diligence and hearing questions is not replaced by the Associates' final diligence findings. He would vacate the report ruling as well as the relationship rulings for the stated inquiry, permits development sufficient to resolve diligence and the material juror/nondisclosure issues, and does not order a writ or unconditional hearing. He joins Part I only and writes separately concerning Parts II and III. Section II does not separately address the independent procedural-default cause ruling; the standing fallback supplies only his vote to reject the lower no-cause ground on the specific concealed-relationship nonpresentation, with no additional cause rationale or authorship eligibility. The fallback does not alter his express diligence-remand choice. Unreviewed components stay outside the grant; no new merits choice is filled.

## Form, assignment and final compatibility

Form was fixed before counting in the separate receipt: one signed opinion in four parts, plus Stone's partial concurrence and partial dissent. Part I states the common statutory construction; Part II affirms the report bar; Part III resolves relationship diligence; Part IV resolves limited cause and preserves remaining conditions. Eight Associates can carry every part, so no split authorship is justified. The Chief assigns Part I and Part IV, whose judgment he supports. Stevens, the senior Associate supporting the precise report affirmance and the final relationship-diligence reversal, assigns Parts II and III. This preserves Stone's distinct vacatur/remand position instead of letting him assign from a determination he withholds. The same eligible author can deliver all parts without altering any coalition.

**Fresh counts before assignment:** Stone-Zsela 5; Stevens 5; O'Connor 6; Scalia 5; Kennedy 5; Souter 5; Thomas 6; Ginsburg 5; Breyer 5. Last counted event: Norfolk Southern Railway Co. v. Shanklin, 2000-04-17; Thomas.

Part I eligible authors and pool: all nine. Parts II–IV eligible authors, and the common full-opinion intersection: Stevens, O'Connor, Scalia, Kennedy, Souter, Thomas, Ginsburg and Breyer. All eight are in the common pool because the minimum is five and no count exceeds six. Stone's Part IV disposition-only fallback does not create eligibility. The Chief and Stevens select Ginsburg for their respective parts. Fit governs the settled grounds: her Lindh and Weeks positions distinguish provision-specific statutory operation, state merits adjudication and factual-development eligibility, the precise separations this opinion must preserve while rejecting the false historical Keeney premise. O'Connor, whose Keeney opinion supplies the former rule, is a strong alternative but has six counted leads to Ginsburg's five and supplies no necessary accommodation absent from the shared ground. Ginsburg can state each claim-specific application and the confined cause rule without importing a general merits construction. One signed plenary lead accrues to Ginsburg after this Record is written; the separate writing does not count.

Final compatibility preserves all eight reconciled commitments, including the express cause clarification and partial-affirmance disposition. Stone's partial join and distinct remand remain visible. No factual finding, statutory exception, cause excuse or automatic hearing is added to attract a join. No draft exchange is invented.

